# Fourteen browser specs, two configs, five hand-written lists (2026-10-05)

`npm run test:e2e` in `apps/chaos-ui` is `node ./e2e-runner.mjs`. That runner loops over a
hand-written list of Playwright config files and hands each to `playwright test --config`;
each config then decides what to collect by matching `testMatch` against the file path. That
is the whole registration mechanism for the browser suite, and both of its failure directions
are quiet: a `*.pw.ts` that no pattern matches is never collected and the run still exits 0,
and a pattern that matches nothing any more is never reported either.

This batch adds `scripts/ci/check-e2e-registration.py`, which reads those lists and the file
names on disk and fails on a spec nothing collects, a project collecting nothing, a name no
spec satisfies any more, and a config no runner names. `docs/ci-test-debt.md` had recorded the
gap on 2026-10-05 as the one left open by the batch that added the second config; that
paragraph is rewritten here to say what closed it.

Everything quoted below is copied verbatim out of runs on 2026-10-05. The Playwright
measurements in section 1 come from the copy of Playwright the repository pins, not from
documentation.

== 0. What is in this batch

    scripts/ci/check-e2e-registration.py          new gate, 734 lines
    scripts/ci/test-check-e2e-registration.py     new fixtures, 500 lines, 40 cases
    .github/workflows/ci.yml                      runs both, in the guards job
    scripts/verify-in-docker.sh                   gates array gains "e2e registration"
    apps/chaos-ui/README.md                       the rule, next to how to run the suite
    docs/ci-test-debt.md                          the gap rewritten as closed, plus the lesson
    CHANGELOG.md, TODO.md                         entries for the gate

The gate is deliberately a reader of text, not a runner of browsers: everything it judges is a
fact about two lists and a set of file names, which is why it can run in the container that
has no browser, and why it costs milliseconds in both roots.

== 1. What Playwright actually does, measured rather than assumed

Every rule the gate enforces is a claim about collection. Those claims were measured with the
Playwright the repository installs, over throwaway projects whose `node_modules` is a symlink
to `apps/chaos-ui/node_modules`, using `--list` so nothing starts a browser or a host. Each
case below prints the config, the files in `testDir`, the exit code and what was collected.

    $ python3 {SCRATCH}/b15-playwright-semantics.py
    playwright --version: Version 1.63.0

    == A. one config, testMatch names alpha only; beta sits in the same directory
        | export default defineConfig({
        |   testDir: './e2e',
        |   testMatch: /alpha\.pw\.ts/,
        | })
        files:  alpha.pw.ts, beta.pw.ts
        exit 0
          collected: {"alpha.pw.ts": ["alpha"]}

    == B. top level matches both, every project sets its own testMatch naming only alpha
        | export default defineConfig({
        |   testDir: './e2e',
        |   testMatch: /(?:alpha|beta)\.pw\.ts/,
        |   projects: [
        |     {
        |       name: 'desktop',
        |       testMatch: /alpha\.pw\.ts/,
        |     },
        |     {
        |       name: 'mobile',
        |       testMatch: /alpha\.pw\.ts/,
        |     },
        |   ],
        | })
        files:  alpha.pw.ts, beta.pw.ts
        exit 0
          collected: {"alpha.pw.ts": ["alpha", "alpha"]}

    == C. a project whose testMatch names a spec that is no longer there
        files:  alpha.pw.ts
        exit 0
          collected: {"alpha.pw.ts": ["alpha"]}

    == D. no testMatch anywhere, so Playwright's own default pattern decides
        files:  alpha.pw.ts, beta.pw.ts
        exit 1
          collected: {}
          error: Error: No tests found

    == E. testMatch as a glob string instead of a pattern
        files:  alpha.pw.ts, beta.pw.ts
        exit 0
          collected: {"alpha.pw.ts": ["alpha"]}

    == F. every project in the config collects nothing
        files:  alpha.pw.ts
        exit 1
          collected: {}
          error: Error: No tests found
    (config text elided for cases C through F; each is printed in full by the driver)

Five things follow, and the gate's rule set is built out of them.

- - - A is the reason the gate exists: `beta.pw.ts` is in the `testDir`, on disk, valid, and
  was collected by nobody, with exit 0. Nothing in a green run distinguishes it from a spec
  that ran. - B is the shape `playwright.config.ts` actually has. The top level matches
  `beta`; both projects set their own `testMatch`, and a project's list replaces the top level
  rather than narrowing it, so `beta` is collected by nobody and the run still exits 0. In the
  repository today the three project lists and the top-level list are identical strings, which
  is why nothing is broken -- and why re-typing one of five copies is the mistake most likely
  to be made. - C is silent too: a project that collects nothing is reported only if no
  project at all finds anything, which is what F shows (exit 1, `No tests found`). So "a
  viewport variant matches no spec" is invisible exactly when the rest of the suite is green.
  - D shows the default pattern does not match `*.pw.ts`. A config with no `testMatch`
  therefore collects nothing, and this is the one case in the list that Playwright itself
  complains about. - E shows a glob `testMatch` works. The gate refuses to read one rather
  than guess at it, so the finding it prints says `this reader understands a regex literal, an
  array of them, or a const naming one. Extend it rather than let a pattern go unread` -- a
  limit of the reader, not a claim that globs are broken.

== 2. The gate against the repository as it stands

    $ python3 scripts/ci/check-e2e-registration.py
    check-e2e-registration: OK (14 spec file(s), each claimed by exactly one of 2 config(s) that e2e-runner.mjs names)

`--list` prints the map the gate built, one spec per line, tab-separated, naming the project
that selects it. It is the answer to "would this file run", which is the question the suite
had no way to ask before.

