    e2e/workspace-flow.pw.ts  playwright.config.ts (project `desktop-chromium`) + playwright.config.ts (project `mobile-chromium`)

Spec names come from the filesystem, not from git's index, because Playwright reads the
filesystem. `e2e/support/` holds helpers and a mock provider endpoint; none of it matches
`*.pw.ts`, and the gate's spec scan skips `node_modules` and dot-directories the way
Playwright's own scan does.

== 3. The four mistakes, injected into a mirror of the real tree

The fixtures in section 4 use miniature configs. This section is the same shipped gate pointed
at a copy of the real `apps/chaos-ui` -- same two configs, same runner, same 14 spec names --
with one fact changed per scenario, so the findings are quoted against the files this
repository actually has. The working tree was never touched: the copy lives outside the
repository and each scenario rebuilds it, and `git status --porcelain` after the run lists
only this batch's own files.

    $ python3 {SCRATCH}/b15-realcopy.py
    scenario: the copy on its own
        exit 0
        check-e2e-registration: OK (14 spec file(s), each claimed by exactly one of 2 config(s) that e2e-runner.mjs names)

    === one spec file nothing names is added
        exit 1
        check-e2e-registration: FAIL (1 problem(s))
          e2e/never-registered.pw.ts: no config claims it; the patterns on the table were playwright.config.ts pattern /(?:workspace-flow|tool-activity|approved-workspace-ops|static-host|reconnect-snapshot|attachment-upload|settings-panel|turn-grouping|streaming-integrity|phone-shell|diff-undo)\.pw\.ts/; playwright.config.ts pattern /approval-competition\.pw\.ts/; playwright.git.config.ts pattern /commit-message(?:-safe-mode)?\.pw\.ts/. It would never run, and the run would exit 0

    === a name every project drops stays at the top level
        exit 1
        check-e2e-registration: FAIL (1 problem(s))
          e2e/streaming-integrity.pw.ts: the top-level `testMatch` of playwright.config.ts matches it, but every project in that config sets its own `testMatch`, which replaces the top level, so the config collects nothing for it

    === the runner stops naming one config
        exit 1
        check-e2e-registration: FAIL (1 problem(s))
          playwright.git.config.ts: nothing runs it -- e2e-runner.mjs lists playwright.config.ts -- so its specs are absent from `npm run test:e2e`, which is what CI types

    === two configs name the same spec
        exit 1
        check-e2e-registration: FAIL (1 problem(s))
          e2e/workspace-flow.pw.ts: claimed by playwright.config.ts and playwright.git.config.ts; `npm run test:e2e` runs every config in turn, so this spec would run twice, against two different hosts

The second scenario is the one worth reading. It deletes `streaming-integrity|` from the two
project lists and leaves the top-level list intact, which is precisely the edit a careful
person makes after misreading the precedence: the name is still in the file, still matches,
and selects nothing. The third is what would have happened when `playwright.git.config.ts` was
added, had `e2e-runner.mjs` not been updated in the same breath.

== 4. Fixtures

    $ python3 scripts/ci/test-check-e2e-registration.py 2>&1 | tail -4
    ----------------------------------------------------------------------
    Ran 40 tests in 1.351s

    OK

`Fixtures` builds a miniature `apps/chaos-ui` in a temporary directory -- two configs with the
same structure as the real ones, a runner whose `configs` array is a literal, a `package.json`
-- and runs the shipped gate as a subprocess against it, so every assertion is about the real
entry point and its real exit code and text. `TextReader` drives the shipped text reader
directly, because a regex-in-JS reader has failure modes that no config-level fixture can
reach.

The structure is copied, including the two things a naive scan trips over: a `webServer` entry
containing `url: \`http://127.0.0.1:${uiPort}/health\`` whose `//` is not a comment, and
`testMatch: gitSpec`, a pattern reached only through a `const`. Cases are grouped by what they
protect:

- - - the real repository: `test_the_real_repository_is_clean_and_actually_scanned` runs the
  shipped gate against this checkout and asserts it passes, that at least 14 specs were read,
  that none is reported unclaimed, and that three named specs are among them. A gate whose
  scan directory was wrong would report a spotless empty suite; this case is what stops that
  being a pass. - one spec, one owner: an unclaimed spec names it and lists the patterns on
  the table; a spec outside every `testDir` says so instead; two configs claiming one spec
  name both. - the precedence trap, plus its control: the trap fires when every project
  replaces the top level, and the control case keeps it from firing when a project inherits. -
  a project that collects nothing, a stale pattern, and a stale alternative inside a live
  pattern (reported by name, because `(?:a|b|c)` losing one branch leaves the pattern alive).
  - the reader's own limits: a glob is refused, an unbound `const` is refused, a `const` used
  before its binding is refused (JavaScript's temporal dead zone), an uncompilable pattern is
  reported, an unknown regex flag is reported rather than dropped, `node_modules` and helper
  files are not specs. - comments: a `testMatch` inside `//` or inside `/* */` registers
  nothing, and a block comment does not shift line numbers, because the finding points at a
  line a person has to open. - the runner and the script: a config the runner does not name, a
  runner naming a file that is not there, the same config listed twice, a `test:e2e` that
  bypasses the runner, and a package with no `test:e2e` at all. - fail-closed: a tree with no
  specs, and a tree with no configs, are both errors. A scan that found nothing must not
  report success, because the symptom of the gate going blind is the green run it exists to
  doubt.

