M23 is recorded as surviving on purpose. The case written to catch it asserted that
`strip_js_comments` returns a character class unchanged -- which is true whether or not the
regex scanner understands the class, because that function copies regex literals wholesale.
The assertion was rewritten against the function that actually decides, `Config._read_regex`,
so it says what the pattern's source is rather than that a copy round-tripped:

    $ python3 {SCRATCH}/mutate-e2e-registration-23.py
    baseline: Ran 40 tests in 1.402s / OK
    KILLED   M23 slash inside a character class ends the pattern
              blind to: [a/] truncates the pattern, so the suffix is lost
              FAIL: test_a_regex_character_class_holds_a_slash (__main__.TextReader)
    KILLED   M25 character-class tracking removed from the regex scanner
              blind to: a `[` no longer opens a class, so a `]` cannot close one either
              FAIL: test_a_regex_character_class_holds_a_slash (__main__.TextReader)
    after restoring: Ran 40 tests in 1.383s / OK (exit 0)

M25 is the other side of the same line: if a `[` does not open a class, a `]` cannot close
one, and the scanner has to fail somewhere. Twenty-five mutants, zero survivors at that point.

The last mutant came out of writing this file. The unclaimed-spec finding originally listed
every pattern in the suite, which on the real configs means the same 11-name list printed
three times, once per place it is re-typed -- three lines of noise burying the two patterns
that differ. The finding was changed to name each distinct pattern once, and the existing
fixture was strengthened to pin that, because nothing had ever asserted the shape of that
message. M26 puts the duplicates back:

    $ python3 {SCRATCH}/mutate-e2e-registration-26.py
    baseline: Ran 40 tests in 1.432s / OK
    M26 duplicated pattern list in the unclaimed finding
              blind to: a finding that repeats one pattern three times reads as three
              FAIL: test_a_spec_named_by_nothing_fails_and_names_it (__main__.Fixtures) / Ran 40 tests in 1.418s / FAILED (failures=1)
    killed
    after restoring: Ran 40 tests in 1.384s / OK (exit 0)

Total: 26 mutants, 26 killed, every source restored byte-identically and confirmed by `cmp`.
Two of the 26 (M23 and M25) exist because the first version of the case written to catch M23
was insensitive, and the honest reading of that is the same lesson as before: a mutation is
only worth what the surviving assertion can see, so the assertion has to be aimed at the
function that makes the decision.

== 6. Fail-closed, and the hole that closing found

A gate that cannot read its subject has to say so. The list of things that are errors rather
than skips: no specs anywhere, no configs, a `testMatch` that is neither a regex literal, an
array of them, nor a `const` naming one (which is why a glob is refused, although E in section
1 shows globs work), an unresolvable or late-bound `const`, an uncompilable pattern, an
unknown regex flag, a missing `--ui-dir` (exit 2). The reason is the specific asymmetry of
this subject: every failure mode of the suite looks like a passing run, so a green light from
a gate that read nothing is exactly the evidence that is worthless.

Writing the fixtures is what turned up a real one. The flag scan read `[gimsuy]*`, but
JavaScript also defines `d` (hasIndices), so `/x\.pw\.ts/d` was being read as a pattern with
no flags: the compiled behaviour was right for this repository's patterns and wrong as a
matter of fact, and the finding a person would need -- "this reader does not know what `d`
means, so it is not judging this pattern" -- never fired. `[dgimsuvy]*` is now read, and
anything outside `imsuvy` is reported. M22 kills the narrow version; it is the only mutant in
the battery that was found by a fixture being written rather than by a defect being reported.

== 7. Both runners, and the wiring that says they agree

The gate is wired on executable lines in `.github/workflows/ci.yml` (guards job) and in the
`gates` array of `scripts/verify-in-docker.sh`. The host runner parses that array rather than
keeping its own copy, so one entry becomes a gate in three places: CI, the container, and
`scripts/verify-gates.sh` on the host.

    $ python3 scripts/ci/check-guard-wiring.py
    check-guard-wiring: OK (72 files in scripts/ci/, 71 reachable, 67 run by scripts/verify-in-docker.sh, 4 recorded CI-only, 1 exempt)

    $ bash scripts/verify-gates.sh --only "e2e registration"
    == gates extracted from /home/chaos/hunt/dev/projects/chaos-code/scripts/verify-in-docker.sh
       host run: the ${bootstrap} prefix is dropped, build gates are skipped
       a skipped build gate plus a changed Rust file is a NOT COVERED failure, not a pass
       env: RUST_MIN_STACK=16777216 (mirrors the container's --env; set it to override)
       --only is in effect (1 pattern(s)); this is a filtered run, not a sweep
    PASS  e2e registration

    all gates passed on the host (1 run, 0 skipped, 1 of 40 selected by --only)

The container run is the one that matters more, because the container is where a fresh clone
is judged, and this container has no browser. The gate's subject is two lists and a set of
file names, so it needs nothing else, and it reads the same checkout through `/src`.

    $ scripts/verify-in-docker.sh --only "e2e registration"
