    CONTAINER EXIT: 0

The tree line is worth reading twice, because this run was made before the commit and the
entry point says so rather than implying otherwise: `4 path(s) differ from HEAD`, naming the
two new files and the two wiring edits. The gate's inputs are `apps/chaos-ui` and nothing
else, and no file under it was among those four, which is why a run on that working tree still
says something about the commit that followed. The container counts 38 gates and the host
counts 40: four of the host's entries are recorded CI-only or host-only, and that difference
is itself reported by `check-guard-wiring.py` and by
`check-workflow-{yaml,shells,toolchain}.py`, all three of which were re-run and pass.

== 8. What the gate does not judge

- - - `testIgnore`, `grep`, `grepInvert`, `shard`, and the positional and `--grep` filters of
  `playwright test` are outside it. The rule is about registration, not about every way a test
  can fail to run. - A config that builds `testMatch` at runtime (from `process.env`, from a
  loop) is refused rather than judged. That refusal is a finding, so the day someone writes
  one, the gate says which file and which line, and the reader gets extended or the config
  gets simplified. - The gate asserts a spec is *collected*. Whether the tests inside it then
  pass is the browser suite's business, which runs in its own CI job and cannot run in this
  container: there is no browser here. - Two specs whose patterns could both match one path is
  a double claim and a failure, but two patterns that could match two different files are not
  compared for overlap. A file is judged against the patterns, never the other way around.
